Patch 0079: the IOP interpreter pre-decodes its instructions.
What: (1) IopMemory keeps a table of pre-decoded instructions for main RAM, one entry per word, filled on first
execution and invalidated by markOwned() (every RAM write: guest stores, module loads, SIF/SPU2 DMA, zeroing)
and reset(); (2) executeDecoded() runs from the entry and dispatches with one flat switch on the handler index
(64+funct SPECIAL, 128+rt REGIMM, 192+rs COP0) instead of nested switches, bodies unchanged; (3) read/write 8/16/32
hit main RAM inline (one range check on the masked address) and fall back to the *Slow generic path for
everything else; (4) runCpu runs ordinary stretches in a tight loop that leaves to the general step() as soon as a
DMA completion cycle, a guest callback, an interrupt, a sentinel/non-RAM pc or a `jr ra` is at hand, so events
happen at the same cycles.
Verified: linux-dev build only so far (no state-hash or timing run yet).
Original to this project.

